<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ report_title }} {{ date }}</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9/dist/leaflet.css" crossorigin="">
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9/dist/leaflet.js" crossorigin=""></script>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.24.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" crossorigin="">
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.24.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js" crossorigin=""></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.1.4/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js" crossorigin=""></script>
<style>{{ css | safe }}</style>
<style>
.provenance-line { color:#6c757d; font-size:.85rem; margin:4px 0 12px }
.half-heading { margin:24px 0 8px; padding-bottom:4px; border-bottom:2px solid #dee2e6 }
.denominator-note { background:#f8f9fa; border-left:3px solid #6c757d; padding:8px 12px;
  font-size:.85rem; color:#495057; margin:8px 0 12px }
.recon-warning { background:#fff3cd; border-left:3px solid #ffc107; padding:8px 12px;
  font-size:.85rem; color:#856404; margin:8px 0 }
.sev-chip { color:#fff; padding:1px 7px; border-radius:10px; font-size:.7rem;
  text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.03em }
.rule-id { font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:.75rem; color:#6c757d }
.attr-name { font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:.8rem }
.dist-line { font-size:.8rem; color:#495057; margin:4px 0 }
.dist-chip { display:inline-block; background:#e9ecef; border-radius:3px; padding:1px 6px; margin:1px 2px;
  max-width:100%; overflow-wrap:anywhere; word-break:break-word }
.delta-line { font-size:.8rem; margin:3px 0 }
.delta-chip { display:inline-block; font-size:.72rem; font-weight:700; border-radius:3px;
  padding:1px 6px; margin-left:4px; font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums }
.delta-add { background:#d1e7dd; color:#0f5132 }
.delta-chg { background:#ffe5d0; color:#7a3e00 }
.delta-rem { background:#f8d7da; color:#842029 }
.status-summary { display:inline-flex; align-items:center; gap:10px; font-size:.78rem;
  color:#495057; font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums }
.status-item { display:inline-flex; align-items:center }
.grid2 { display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:16px }
@media (max-width:820px) { .grid2 { grid-template-columns:1fr } }
.osw-map { height:520px; margin:12px 0; border-radius:6px }
.timeline { display:flex; align-items:flex-end; gap:2px; height:120px;
  border-bottom:1px solid #dee2e6; padding-bottom:2px }
.timeline-day { flex:1; min-width:3px; height:100%; display:flex; align-items:flex-end }
.timeline-stack { width:100%; display:flex; flex-direction:column-reverse;
  border-radius:2px 2px 0 0; overflow:hidden; min-height:2px }
.osw-modal-backdrop { display:none; position:fixed; inset:0; background:rgba(0,0,0,.5);
  z-index:299 }
.osw-modal { display:none; position:fixed; top:50%; left:50%;
  transform:translate(-50%,-50%); z-index:400; background:#fff; border-radius:8px;
  max-width:760px; width:92%; max-height:82vh; overflow-y:auto; padding:24px }
.osw-modal.open, .osw-modal-backdrop.open { display:block }
.osw-modal h2 { margin-top:0 }
.osw-modal code { background:#f1f3f5; padding:1px 5px; border-radius:3px; font-size:.85em }
.osw-modal-close { position:sticky; top:0; float:right; border:none; background:none;
  font-size:1.5rem; cursor:pointer; color:#6c757d }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<h1>{{ report_title }}</h1>
<p class="subtitle">{{ date }} &nbsp;·&nbsp; {{ workspaces }}</p>
{{ body | safe }}

<div class="osw-modal-backdrop"></div>
<div class="osw-modal">
  <button class="osw-modal-close" aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
  <h2>Methodology</h2>

  <h3>Where the numbers come from</h3>
  <p>Every figure derives from the <strong>full before/after record</strong> of each
  changeset — the complete state of every element the changeset touched, both before
  and after the edit. No statistical model, no language model, no external inference.
  Re-running the report on the same cache always produces the same output.</p>

  <h3>What counts as an edit</h3>
  <p>That record also includes <em>context rows</em>: elements the changeset merely
  referenced, such as a way whose child node moved. An action is counted only when the
  element's own <code>changeset</code> matches the changeset being examined. Counting
  context rows would inflate edit volume by roughly a third and credit work to whoever
  last touched the element rather than whoever made the change.</p>
  <p>As a check, the counted actions are reconciled against each changeset's reported
  <code>changes_count</code>. If they disagree, the report says so at the top rather than
  showing a plausible wrong number.</p>

  <h3>Two different units</h3>
  <p><strong>What changed &amp; who changed it</strong> counts <em>actions</em> — one
  element edited twice counts twice, because that is two pieces of work.
  <strong>Resulting data state</strong> counts <em>distinct elements in their final
  state</em>. Neither is wrong; they answer different questions.</p>

  <h3>The denominator</h3>
  <p>Completeness is measured over elements these changesets <strong>touched</strong> and
  that still exist — not the whole workspace. A figure of 100% means "complete among the
  elements that were edited", never "complete across the city". Run with
  <code>--full-extract</code> to widen the denominator to every raw element — including
  untagged geometry vertices, not just OSW features — within the <strong>edited
  extent</strong>: the bounding box spanning these changesets, fetched via
  <code>/api/0.6/map</code>. This is still <em>not</em> the whole workspace — anything
  outside that box is absent — and it is a raw element count, not a feature count. The
  denominator note above always states which is in use.</p>

  <h3>The baseline import</h3>
  <p>The initial TDEI import is excluded by default. On a typical workspace it accounts
  for the large majority of all actions, so including it would drown out the human
  editing the report exists to describe. It is still listed in the provenance line, and
  <code>--include-baseline</code> folds it in.</p>

  <h3>Two families of finding</h3>
  <p><strong>Conformance</strong> findings say the data disagrees with the OpenSidewalks
  schema — a placeholder value, a value outside a defined enumeration, a required
  attribute absent. These are fixable in the data.</p>
  <p><strong>Network</strong> findings say the data is correct and what it describes is
  worth attention — for example crossings recorded as unmarked. These are not data
  defects; fixing them means changing the world, not the database.</p>

  <h3>Every rule's predicate</h3>
  <p>Each finding names the rule id that produced it. What each id actually tests:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>width-placeholder</code> — a populated <code>width</code> value equals the
    placeholder <code>0</code>/<code>0.0</code>.</li>
    <li><code>enum-off-schema:&lt;attr&gt;</code> — a populated enum attribute (one rule
    instance per enum attribute — <code>surface</code>, <code>tactile_paving</code>,
    <code>crossing:markings</code>, <code>foot</code>, <code>climb</code>) holds a value
    outside the set that attribute's schema entry declares.</li>
    <li><code>steps-no-count</code> — a Steps entity (<code>highway=steps</code>) has no
    <code>step_count</code> tag.</li>
    <li><code>crossing-unmarked</code> — a Crossing entity has
    <code>crossing:markings=no</code>.</li>
    <li><code>curb-type-mix</code> — tally of which curb entity (Curb Ramp, Raised/Rolled/
    Flush/Generic Curb) each live, schema-recognized curb feature matched.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>So any number here can be traced back to an exact predicate and argued with.</p>
</div>

<script>{{ js | safe }}</script>
</body>
</html>
